Fix Hyprland 0.56.0 aborting when a monitor is powered back on.

Switching a display off with its power button drops HPD, so Hyprland tears the
monitor down; switching it back on re-adds it, and the DRM atomic tests for
every mode are rejected while the panel is still coming up ("REJECTED available
mode", "has NO FALLBACK MODES"). CMonitor::applyMonitorRule() returns early on
that path: the monitor stays enabled and listed in State::monitorState()->
monitors(), with m_transformedSize still 0x0, while scheduleModeRetry() retries
once per second.

Anything that reloads config or renames/renumbers a workspace - both of which
happen while workspaces are shuffled back onto the returning monitor - trips
REFRESH_ALL, and CPropRefresher::refreshProp() then calls resources() on every
monitor in that list. For the zero-sized one that constructs CMonitorResources
with size 0x0, so CMonitorResources::initFB() hits
  RASSERT((w > 0 && h > 0), "cannot alloc a FB with negative / zero size!")
in Render::IFramebuffer::alloc() and the compositor dies with SIGABRT, taking
the whole session with it.

Skip monitors without a usable size, exactly like IHyprRenderer::renderMonitor()
already refuses to render them. Nothing is lost: a successful modeset resets
m_resources and sets m_blurFBDirty, so the blur FB is rebuilt at the real size.

--- a/src/config/supplementary/propRefresher/PropRefresher.cpp
+++ b/src/config/supplementary/propRefresher/PropRefresher.cpp
@@ -80,12 +80,21 @@ void CPropRefresher::refreshProp(const bool execdAsScheduled) {
         g_pHyprRenderer->refreshBlurProvider();
 
         for (auto const& m : State::monitorState()->monitors()) {
             if (!m)
                 continue;
 
+            // A monitor whose modeset failed is still enabled and listed here while
+            // CMonitor::scheduleModeRetry() retries, but has a 0x0 transformed size (e.g. a
+            // display that was just switched back on and has not published a usable mode
+            // yet). resources() would allocate its FBs at that size and RASSERT in
+            // IFramebuffer::alloc(), taking the whole compositor down. Skipping is safe: a
+            // successful modeset drops m_resources and marks the blur FB dirty anyway.
+            if (m->m_transformedSize.x < 1 || m->m_transformedSize.y < 1)
+                continue;
+
             m->resources()->refreshBlurFB();
 
             if (*PBLURENABLED) {
                 m->m_blurFBDirty     = true;
                 m->m_forceFullFrames = 2;
                 m->scheduleFrame();
